15	    /// Require `var` declarations to appear at the top of their containing scope.
16	    ///
17	    /// Because `var` declarations are hoisted to the top of the nearest function,
18	    /// script, module, or static block, placing them later in the body makes code
19	    /// harder to follow. Keeping them at the top makes the scope's variable
20	    /// declarations easier to find. Note that this is not a problem for `let` and
21	    /// `const` declarations, which are block-scoped and not hoisted.
22	    ///
23	    /// This rule only allows leading standalone `var` statements. At module
24	    /// scope, leading `export var` declarations are allowed too. Directives and
25	    /// imports may appear before them.
26	    ///
27	    /// ## Examples
28	    ///
29	    /// ### Invalid
30	    ///
31	    /// ```js,expect_diagnostic
32	    /// function f() {
33	    ///     doSomething();
34	    ///     var value = 1;
35	    /// }
36	    /// ```
37	    ///
38	    /// ### Valid
39	    ///
40	    /// ```js
41	    /// function f() {
42	    ///     var value = 1;
43	    ///     doSomething(value);
44	    /// }
45	    /// ```
46	    ///
